\section*{Approximation paving for every MASA}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
Every self-adjoint element is claimed to admit strong approximants bounded by three times its norm, norm-pavable over any MASA with O(\(\varepsilon^{-6}\)) pieces and error relative to each approximant's norm. The cited manuscript argues for that result; its relation to this problem is specified below.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Manuscript 1 (family 300, 2026-09-25): Buffered determinant polynomials give measurable one-sided matrix pavings. For the normalizer algebra these transfer through measured equivalence relations, while random diagonal phases treat diffuse complementary kernels. Separable reduction and a three-corner assembly remove the expectation hypothesis; norming vectors and clipping retain the required normalization for the actual approximants.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Manuscript 1 (family 300, 2026-09-25): The problem selects the mathematical statement summarized here, including its stated hypotheses and parameter range. The cited theorem gives a claimed proof of that assertion in full.

\subsection{Formal verification}
Family 300 has no Lean scope page in this snapshot; no formalization of these manuscript results is listed.

This site has not executed the Lean files or checked their dependencies. These descriptions report the scope of the linked declarations, rather than a new verification of the proofs.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these results to an internal model. Its release notice cautions that results lacking full formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently assessed the proofs; a solved label records the scope of the reported claim.

